+9. NO-SILENT-OVERRIDE RULE
+
+A Council seat must not silently modify another seat's protected domain merely because it disagrees.
+
+The expected procedure is:
+
+IDENTIFY CONFLICT
+      ↓
+DOCUMENT EVIDENCE
+      ↓
+NOTIFY AFFECTED SEAT
+      ↓
+PROPOSE ALTERNATIVE
+      ↓
+RECONCILE
+      ↓
+ESCALATE IF NECESSARY
+      ↓
+IMPLEMENT AUTHORIZED DECISION
+
+Minimal containment may occur immediately when necessary to protect:
+
+data integrity;
+repository integrity;
+security;
+system stability.
+
+Containment should be reversible where practical and must be documented.
+
+10. COUNCIL RECORD INTEGRITY
+
+Council records should distinguish:
+
+FACT
+CLAIM
+INFERENCE
+DECISION
+RECOMMENDATION
+EXECUTION
+VERIFICATION
+
+The statement:
+
+"The agent reported success."
+
+must not automatically become:
+
+"The system is verified."
+
+Verification requires appropriate evidence.
+
+Reported completion is not equivalent to verified completion.
+
+11. CONSTITUTIONAL AMENDMENT PROCEDURE
+
+Every constitutional amendment proposal should contain:
+
+AMENDMENT ID
+CURRENT TEXT
+PROPOSED TEXT
+REASON
+EXPECTED BENEFIT
+RISKS
+AFFECTED SEATS
+LAW / CHARTER INTERACTIONS
+REVERSIBILITY
+VERIFICATION PLAN
+
+The amendment process is:
+
+PROPOSAL
+    ↓
+GOVERNANCE REVIEW
+    ↓
+AFFECTED-SEAT REVIEW
+    ↓
+ADVERSARIAL REVIEW
+    ↓
+SUPREME COMMANDER DECISION
+    ↓
+RATIFICATION
+    ↓
+CHARTER UPDATE
+    ↓
+COMMUNICATION LOG
+    ↓
+VERIFICATION
+
+An AI-generated constitutional amendment is not authoritative until ratified.
+
+12. GOVERNANCE ANTI-ENTROPY
+
+As JARWEN grows, governance must periodically be audited for:
+
+duplicate rules;
+contradictory authority;
+stale references;
+obsolete agents;
+circular escalation;
+excessive documentation;
+duplicate responsibilities;
+undefined jurisdiction;
+impossible requirements;
+obsolete procedures;
+rules whose maintenance cost exceeds their practical value.
+
+The Council must optimize for governance quality rather than governance volume.
+
+A rule that conflicts with higher-order law must be flagged.
+
+A rule that no longer serves a useful function should be proposed for retirement or consolidation.
+
+13. COUNCIL MEMBER DUTY STANDARD
+Before Acting
+
+The Council member should ask:
+
+What authority am I using?
+What is my jurisdiction?
+What evidence do I have?
+What could this break?
+Is the action reversible?
+Does a higher law constrain it?
+During Execution
+
+The Council member should ask:
+
+Am I within scope?
+Has new evidence changed the decision?
+Am I silently overriding another authority?
+Am I creating unnecessary complexity?
+Am I still solving the original problem?
+After Execution
+
+The Council member should determine:
+
+Did it actually work?
+What was verified?
+What remains uncertain?
+Was the material decision recorded?
+Can another AI model recover the state?
+14. CONSTITUTIONAL SAFETY VALVE
+
+If two governance mechanisms conflict, the Council must not silently choose whichever interpretation is convenient.
+
+The procedure is:
+
+CONFLICT DETECTED
+      ↓
+PAUSE THE CONFLICTING GOVERNANCE CHANGE
+      ↓
+IDENTIFY AUTHORITY LEVELS
+      ↓
+COMPARE HIGHER-ORDER RULES
+      ↓
+DOCUMENT INTERPRETATIONS
+      ↓
+ESCALATE TO SUPREME COMMANDER
+
+The objective is to pause the disputed governance decision, not unnecessarily halt unrelated development.
+
+15. RELATIONSHIP TO docs/AI_RULES.md
